#version 450
/*==============================================================================================================================================
                                                              SURFELAGE.COMP
==============================================================================================================================================*/
// 🧩 The surfel ECONOMY (webgiya Age / "Surfel Economy", surfelAgePass.ts). One lane per pool slot, up to the high-water mark PoolMax[0]. It decides
//    which surfels live, how fast they age, and which die — the full four-mechanic balance that keeps coverage in equilibrium instead of oscillating:
//
//      1. POLICE EXECUTION — a surfel whose `touched` income this frame is the KILL signal (255, written by the spawn pass's despawn vote in an
//         over-covered cell) is recycled immediately, no metabolism.
//      2. CROWDING RENT — a surfel in a cell holding more than SAFE_CAP (32) surfels ages FASTER; excess/10 above SAFE_CAP, plus excess²/32 above 2×.
//         Over-subscribed cells thin themselves out; sparse cells age at the base rate.
//      3. KEEP-ALIVE INCOME — a well-covered, well-aligned surfel gets `touched` income (5..50, from the spawn pass's keep-alive write and the
//         gather's importance write). Income is SUBTRACTED from the age delta (`max(delta - income, 0)`), so contributing surfels age slower or not at
//         all. This is what STAGGERS death: a cohort that all spawned together no longer all recycles at frame 500 in lockstep — the ones still pulling
//         their weight keep resetting their metabolism while the idle ones age out, which is the fix for the breathing limit cycle.
//      4. BASE METABOLISM + TTL — the surviving delta is +1/frame; when age reaches TTL the surfel is DECLARED DEAD, its age stamped
//         SURFEL_LIFE_RECYCLED, and its slot pushed back onto the free-list (atomicAdd(-1) on the stack pointer, write the slot at the freed position).
//
//    🔴 `touched` income is READ-AND-CLEARED with atomicMin(touched, 0): the values are all non-negative so atomicMin(·,0) returns the old value and
//       leaves 0 behind, draining the mailbox each frame exactly as webgiya does (surfelAgePass.ts:110). The rent count comes from the grid Offsets
//       slice for the surfel's own cell, hashed from its position relative to the SNAPPED grid origin the spawn/slotting used this frame (Push.GridOrigin).
//
//    🔴 Dispatched over the pool CAPACITY (one lane per slot, gated by PoolMax[0]), NOT an indirect count. webgiya drives Age with an indirect dispatch
//       sized to the live count; Frontier's slotting count/slot passes already dispatch over capacity and gate on PoolMax, so this matches them.

#include "SurfelGrid.glsl"
#include "SurfelRecord.glsl"

// 🩺 Per-frame census (diagnostic only — see SurfelCensus.glsl). Binding 9 on the shared pool set; the tallies below are the DEATH side of the churn
//    measurement, split BY CAUSE because TTL death and police execution imply different fixes. Nothing in this pass reads them back.
#define SURFEL_CENSUS_BINDING 9
#include "SurfelCensus.glsl"

layout(local_size_x = 64) in;

layout(std430, binding = 0) buffer SurfelBuffer    { SurfelRecord Surfels[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 1) buffer PoolBuffer      { int PoolFreeList[]; };
layout(std430, binding = 2) buffer PoolAllocBuffer { int PoolAlloc[]; };   // stack pointer atomic
layout(std430, binding = 3) buffer PoolMaxBuffer   { int PoolMax[]; };     // high-water atomic (read only here)
layout(std430, binding = 7) readonly buffer OffsetsBuffer { int Offsets[]; };   // per-cell slice [start,end) — the rent-crowding count source
layout(std430, binding = 8) buffer TouchedBuffer  { int Touched[]; };      // per-surfel income mailbox: 5..50 keep-alive, 255 kill

layout(push_constant) uniform PushBlock
{
    // 🔴 THIS BLOCK IS SHARED WITH SurfelAllocate.comp AND SurfelPrepare.comp THROUGH ONE PoolPushBlock IN SurfelLifecycleSubmission.cpp. The names here
    //    must sit at the SAME OFFSETS as that struct's — word 1 is TileCount and word 2 is SyncPass to those shaders, so neither is a free pad even
    //    though this shader ignores them. Getting this wrong has no compile error: Age would read Allocate's tile count as its own flag.
    //    📝 HOW AN AGE-ONLY FIELD IS ADDED. Each shader declares a PREFIX of the C++ struct: Prepare stops after Capacity, Allocate stops after Pad0,
    //       only this shader reads GridOrigin and beyond. Declaring fewer TRAILING fields than the host pushes is legal block layout, so an Age-only
    //       field is APPENDED TO THE TAIL of both this block and PoolPushBlock and needs no edit to the other two shaders. Inserting one anywhere ABOVE
    //       GridOrigin would silently re-offset them both.
    int  Capacity;
    int  TileCount;    // [-] - Allocate's lane bound; UNUSED here, do not repurpose
    int  SyncPass;     // [-] - Allocate's lane selector; UNUSED here, do not repurpose
    int  Pad0;         // [-] - vec4 alignment for GridOrigin; UNUSED here, do not repurpose
    vec4 GridOrigin;   // xyz snapped grid origin (matches the slotting/spawn origin this frame); w unused
    // 🔴 THE LIVE WORLD SCALE, WHICH THIS PASS WAS MISSING ENTIRELY — AND THE CROWDING RENT HAS BEEN READING THE WRONG CELL SINCE IT SHIPPED.
    //    SurfelHashOfPosition divides by g_SurfelCellDiameter (SurfelGrid.glsl:90), and every other pass that hashes seats the live value through
    //    SurfelSetTuning first: Count, Slot, Integrate and SpawnRequest all do. Age was the ONE hashing pass that did not, so whenever the
    //    F10 cell-diameter slider sat away from the baked SURFEL_GRID_CELL_DIAMETER default, this pass hashed each surfel into a DIFFERENT cell than the
    //    slotting that built the Offsets array — and then read that cell's occupancy as its rent. Wrong cell, plausible number, no error anywhere: rent
    //    was charged against a stranger's crowding. It stayed invisible because rent only nudges an age delta, so the symptom was a slightly-off
    //    population rather than anything that looks like a lookup fault.
    //    CameraPosition is the RAW eye, deliberately NOT the snapped GridOrigin above: SurfelRadiusForPositionEye measures true eye distance while the
    //    grid indices come from the snapped origin (SurfelGrid.glsl:160-162). They are different points and swapping them mis-sizes every radius.
    vec4  CameraPosition;    // [m] - xyz raw eye (radius scaling); w unused
    float TuneCellDiameter;  // [m] - live base cell edge; MUST match the value slotting hashed with this frame
    float TuneBaseRadius;    // [m] - live cascade-0 disc radius
} Push;

// SURFEL_TTL and SURFEL_MAX_SURFELS_PER_CELL come from SurfelGrid.glsl (included above) — do not redefine them here.
const int SURFEL_LIFE_RECYCLE  = 0x8000000;
const int SURFEL_LIFE_RECYCLED = 0x8000000 + 1;

// 📝 The floor on the per-frame age delta for a PAID surfel — the smallest amount income can leave uncancelled. 1 means a fully-paid surfel ages at
//    the base metabolism, reaching TTL=500 in 500 frames (~8s at 60fps) instead of never; an idle surfel still ages at Delta + rent, so the spread
//    between contributor and freeloader — webgiya's staggering — survives. 0 restores the old uncapped cancel (and the frozen field with it).
const int SURFEL_MIN_AGE_DELTA_WHEN_PAID = 1;

// Economy constants — ported 1:1 from webgiya constants.ts.
const int SURFEL_MAX_SURFELS_PER_CELL_FOR_KEEP_ALIVE = 32;   // SAFE_CAP: a cell over this starts charging rent
const int SURFEL_KILL_SIGNAL                         = 255;  // the despawn vote's kill value in the touched mailbox

void main()
{
    // 🔴 SEAT THE LIVE WORLD SCALE BEFORE ANY HASH OR RADIUS HELPER RUNS — see the push-block note. SurfelHashOfPosition (the crowding rent) reads these
    //    globals, and without this call they hold the baked defaults while the rest of the frame used the F10 values. The near-field bias is spawn-only,
    //    so 1.0 (inert) is passed rather than plumbing a field this pass cannot use.
    SurfelSetTuning(Push.TuneCellDiameter, Push.TuneBaseRadius, 1.0);

    int Index = int(gl_GlobalInvocationID.x);
    int Total = PoolMax[0];
    if (Index >= Total) return;

    int Age = Surfels[Index].Age;

    // Drain the touched mailbox for THIS surfel every frame — non-negative values, so atomicMin(·,0) reads the old value and clears to 0.
    int Income = atomicMin(Touched[Index], 0);

    if (Age >= SURFEL_TTL) return;   // already dead / recycled — leave it (mailbox still drained above, matching webgiya)

    int NextAge;
    // 🩺 Census only: WHICH death path ran, so the row can split the causes. Held as the census slot itself so there is no second mapping to keep in sync.
    int DeathCause = SURFEL_CENSUS_DIED_TTL;
    if (Income == SURFEL_KILL_SIGNAL)
    {
        // 1. POLICE EXECUTION — the despawn vote flagged this surfel; kill it now.
        NextAge = SURFEL_LIFE_RECYCLE;
        DeathCause = SURFEL_CENSUS_DIED_POLICE;
    }
    else
    {
        // 2. CROWDING RENT — read this surfel's own cell occupancy from the grid Offsets slice.
        vec3 Relative = Surfels[Index].PositionAndSpare.xyz - Push.GridOrigin.xyz;
        uint Hash     = SurfelHashOfPosition(Relative);
        int  Start    = Offsets[int(Hash)];
        int  End      = Offsets[int(Hash) + 1];
        int  Count    = End - Start;

        int Delta  = 1;   // base metabolism
        int Excess = max(0, Count - SURFEL_MAX_SURFELS_PER_CELL_FOR_KEEP_ALIVE);
        if (Count > SURFEL_MAX_SURFELS_PER_CELL_FOR_KEEP_ALIVE)
            Delta += Excess / 10;                       // linear rent above SAFE_CAP
        if (Count > SURFEL_MAX_SURFELS_PER_CELL_FOR_KEEP_ALIVE * 2)
            Delta += (Excess * Excess) / 32;            // quadratic rent above 2×

        // 3. KEEP-ALIVE INCOME — income cancels the delta; you can stop age, never reverse it.
        // 🔴 THE FLOOR IS WHAT KEEPS THE FIELD FROM FREEZING. Income runs to 50 (SurfelGather.glsl's SURFEL_IMPORTANCE_INDIRECT_MAX) against a base
        //    metabolism of 1, so an UNCAPPED cancel makes any surfel that wins a gather immortal. Measured: the field pinned at 864 surfels 14 frames
        //    in and requested almost nothing for the next 1000 frames — immortal incumbents keep CellAlreadyCovered true, which suppresses the
        //    challengers that would replace them, which keeps the incumbents winning. A closed loop with no camera term, so moving the view never
        //    breaks it and coverage falls behind the eye. webgiya can afford the uncapped cancel because its despawn vote evicts the incumbent from
        //    outside the loop; this path has no vote (diedPolice = 0 by construction), so the ONLY remaining eviction is age reaching TTL.
        //    Flooring the delta at 1 keeps webgiya's staggering intact — a paid surfel still ages far slower than an idle one, so cohorts do not die
        //    in lockstep — while guaranteeing every surfel reaches TTL in bounded time and re-opens its cell to a fresh claim.
        NextAge = Age + max(Delta - Income, SURFEL_MIN_AGE_DELTA_WHEN_PAID);
        NextAge = max(0, NextAge);

        // 🩺 Census: this surfel was PAID this frame. Counting the paid population is what shows whether income is reaching the field at all — if
        //    keepAlive tracks alive while diedTtl stays high, income is arriving but cannot outrun the delta (the max(·,0) floor at line above).
        if (Income > 0)
            SurfelCensusTally(SURFEL_CENSUS_KEEP_ALIVE);
    }

    Surfels[Index].Age = NextAge;

    if (NextAge >= SURFEL_TTL)
    {
        // 4. Death: stamp recycled and return the slot to the free-list stack.
        Surfels[Index].Age = SURFEL_LIFE_RECYCLED;
        int FreedSlot = atomicAdd(PoolAlloc[0], -1) - 1;   // value BEFORE the subtract, minus one
        if (FreedSlot >= 0 && FreedSlot < Push.Capacity)
            PoolFreeList[FreedSlot] = Index;

        // 🩺 Census: split the cause. Both paths land here (the police branch stamps SURFEL_LIFE_RECYCLE, which is far above SURFEL_TTL), so the branch's
        //    recorded cause is the only thing that still knows which one ran. The split is the point: TTL deaths say the ECONOMY is killing probes on a
        //    timer (fixable only in this pass), police deaths say the SPAWN pass is over-covering cells (fixable by better placement).
        SurfelCensusTally(DeathCause);
    }
}
